# DESCRIPTION

Section 7 explains the ideas the rest of the manual takes for granted, as Plan 9's papers did: namespaces and how they are made; rings, which carry 9Px and bulk data between processes; how time and waiting work; and how the system is installed and updated.
A reader new to the system starts here, before the reference pages of the other sections.

The pages, in the order a newcomer might read them:

: services(7)
  How the system comes up and runs: Limine, the kernel, svcd and its manifests, devmgr and the drivers.
: namespace(7)
  Per-process namespaces: names, mount and bind, unions, `/srv`, and how a process gets its own.
: handles(7)
  Kernel objects, handles and their rights, what a service is granted, and users.
: rings(7)
  Channels and rings, which carry 9Px and bulk data between processes.
: sharing(7)
  Structured data shared without serialising: VMOs, mappings, file mappings and open files.
: notes(7)
  Notes, note groups, signals, exceptions and crash directories.
: time(7)
  The clock, deadlines, futexes, ports and the scheduler.
: storage(7)
  Disks, the system volume, branches and snapshots, and the other file systems.
: updates(7)
  Releases, the content store, installing, boot slots, apply and rollback.
: posix(7)
  The POSIX environment: musl on VectraOS, sbase's commands, and what is missing.
: manual(7)
  The manual itself: its sections, its format, its index and its check.

Each explains what is built now; what the design documents in `docs/` plan beyond it is in its BUGS.

